theorem:
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:inv}
Under those assumptions, every $|x'-x|\le\varepsilon$ satisfies $\mathrm{proj}(x')=x'$ and $\mathrm{proj}(x)=x$. Sharpness: if some ball point is infeasible, the ball-feasibility hypothesis fails.
\end{theorem}

examples:
\begin{example}
If $\mathrm{InSet}=[-1,1]$, $x=0$, $\varepsilon=0.25$, every $x'$ in the ball is feasible and fixed by identity projection.
\end{example}

